# StarExec sudoers configuration
# This file grants minimal, specific privileges to the starexec user
# for job isolation and sandbox management.
#
# SECURITY NOTES:
# - All commands are absolute paths (prevents PATH manipulation)
# - Commands are restricted to specific use cases (no wildcards)
# - Arguments are validated where possible
# - No NOPASSWD on unrestricted commands
# - Logs all usage via syslog

# ============================================================================
# JOB EXECUTION ISOLATION
# ============================================================================
# Allow starexec to execute commands as sandbox users (starexec1, starexec2)
# This is required for job isolation: each job runs as a different UID
#
# Constraints:
#   - Can only become starexec1 or starexec2 (not arbitrary users)
#   - Can only use /bin/bash and /bin/sh (standard shells)
#   - Environment is sanitized (SETENV not allowed)
#
# Usage: sudo -u starexec1 /bin/bash /path/to/solver.sh
starexec ALL=(starexec1,starexec2) NOPASSWD: /bin/bash, /bin/sh

# ============================================================================
# FILE OWNERSHIP MANAGEMENT
# ============================================================================
# Allow starexec to change ownership of job directories
# NOTE: File ownership is handled by the application via:
#   1. Copying files as the sandbox user (automatic ownership)
#   2. Group permissions (chmod g+rwxs for shared access)
#   3. NOT via sudo chown
#
# The sandboxChownDirectory() method is intentionally commented out.
# See SUDO_ARGUMENT_INJECTION_ANALYSIS.md for detailed threat analysis.
# This rule was removed in v2.2.0 to eliminate the sudo chown footgun.

# ============================================================================
# SECURITY CONSTRAINTS
# ============================================================================
# The following are explicitly NOT allowed:
#
# ❌ NOPASSWD: ALL
#    (Would allow any command as any user)
#
# ❌ ALL=(ALL)
#    (Would allow becoming any user, not just sandbox users)
#
# ❌ NOPASSWD: /bin/sh -c *
#    (Would allow arbitrary shell interpretation)
#
# ❌ !requiretty
#    (Enforces TTY requirement, prevents non-interactive abuse)
#
# ❌ SETENV
#    (Prevents environment variable injection attacks)

# ============================================================================
# LOGGING
# ============================================================================
# All sudo commands are logged to syslog by default.
# Monitor sudo usage:
#   journalctl -u sudo
#   grep sudo /var/log/auth.log
#
# Alert on suspicious patterns:
#   - Multiple failed attempts (indicates brute force or misconfiguration)
#   - Execution of commands outside expected scope
#   - chown to unexpected users (indicates privilege escalation attempt)

# ============================================================================
# MAINTENANCE & AUDITING
# ============================================================================
# When modifying this file:
#
# 1. Test the change in a staging container:
#    docker run --rm -it starexec:test bash
#    sudo -l  # Lists allowed commands
#
# 2. Verify the exact syntax:
#    visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/starexec
#
# 3. Document the change:
#    - Add comment explaining the new rule
#    - Reference the GitHub issue or requirement
#
# 4. Review the change:
#    - Security team approval required
#    - Test in staging environment
#    - Monitor logs after deployment

